# Typing method bodies under single capability bounds

The rules of this design specify the behaviour, not the implementation.

```
body-ok:  forall XBs1 in split(XBs XBs'),  XBs1, G G' |- e : T
split(X1:RCs1 ... Xn:RCsn) = { X1:RC1 ... Xn:RCn | RC1 in RCs1 ... RCn in RCsn }
```

`XBs` are the type parameters of the literal and `XBs'` those of the method.
`G` are the bindings `Lit-ok` gives the method, adapted once by `G[XBs,RC]` under `XBs`; `G'` are the parameters.
Kinding (signatures, supertypes, the capture filter) and the affinity premise use the declared bounds, not an element of the split.

## Nested literals

A literal `RC L` of type `D[Ts]` in a method body is typed by `Lit-t`.
Invariant: the premise `G|FTV(Ts) |- RC L : OK` mentions no bound of the enclosing method, and `G` does not depend on `XBs1`: the premise is the same in every element of the split.
The rest of `Lit-t`, the kinding of `D[Ts]` included, is checked in every element.

## Rules under a single capability bound

- `RC-sub` compares one capability on each side; `ri-sub` (`X <= read/imm X`) follows from `RC-sub` and is no rule of its own.
- Type arguments are compared after writing the capability of each type variable explicitly (`X` becomes `RC X`, `read/imm X` becomes `readImm(RC) X`).
- Promotions apply the mode to the single capability of a type variable.

## Behaviour

Accepted:

```
A:{ .m[Z:imm,readH](a: read/imm Z): Z -> a }
K[Y:iso,mut]:{ imm .m(a: Y): mut Y }
C:{ .c[Z:iso,mut](k: imm K[Z], a: Z): Z -> k.m(a) }
Box[T:*]:{}
Sub:{ .m[X:mut,read](x: Box[read X]): Box[read/imm X] -> x }
```

No program accepted by `main` is rejected.

When some element of the split types the body, an error inside the body names the element it comes from: `While inspecting ".m(_)" line 3, when Y is imm`; an error listing the promotions of a call lists those of that element only.
When every element fails, the frame has no `when` clause.

## Implementation notes

Implementation: branch `split-bounds` of https://github.com/marcoautomation2/Frontend/tree/split-bounds. Paper: version `proposed` of the paper repository, built on version `current`.
In the proof, the lemma Semantic Split combines the typings of the split: a ground substitution compatible with the declared bounds is compatible with exactly one element of the split.
The premise `G|FTV(Ts) |- RC L : OK` of each nested literal is checked once, with an identity set over the literal as written (`_l` in `checkLiteral`); the copy `withRC(iso)` is a new object at every check.
`onlyImmCapture`, used by code generation, is computed under the bounds of the literal, the same in every element of the split.
